    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nI don't think it is necessary to make notification API more complicated in this case. There are two notification \"layers\" at play here:\r\n- Notifications produced by the output of `make_notify()` are essentially [added and then deleted after configurable delay](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/b07157c51e52a1b004ec7959f9618e9bc24686d3/lua/mini/notify.lua#L310-L311). They are not meant to be updated: show immediately and hide after later. It should be not be more complicated (unless documented `vim.notify` interface changes).\r\n- Notifications managed by lower level [`add()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/b07157c51e52a1b004ec7959f9618e9bc24686d3/lua/mini/notify.lua#L310-L311), [`update()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/b07157c51e52a1b004ec7959f9618e9bc24686d3/doc/mini-notify.txt#L286), [`remove()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/b07157c51e52a1b004ec7959f9618e9bc24686d3/doc/mini-notify.txt#L299). Those are specifically designed to be a public interface for users to micromanage notifications without the need to deal with how they are displayed.\r\n\r\nI believe going the second route is what should be preferable here. I.e. manually add, update, and remove notifications based on custom logic (including prolonging time until removal if update was made).\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned.",
            "created_at": "2024-11-18T11:41:45Z",
